<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8" /><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" /><title>Compliant Gripper &amp; Wrist | Daniel Winfield</title><meta name="description" content="Daniel Winfield’s ongoing compliant gripper and wrist development: measured hardware interfaces, prototype CAD, and planned fit checks." /><link rel="stylesheet" href="/styles/research.css" /><link rel="stylesheet" href="/styles/evidence.css" /><SiteHead title={"Compliant Gripper & Wrist | Daniel Winfield"} description={"Daniel Winfield’s ongoing compliant gripper and wrist development: measured hardware interfaces, prototype CAD, and planned fit checks."} path={"/projects/gripper-wrist/"} />
</head><body id="top"><a class="skip-link" href="#main">Skip to content</a><SiteHeader section="projects" /><main id="main"><section class="research-hero"><div><p class="eyebrow">Mechanical Design · Component Fit · Prototyping</p><h1>Compliant Gripper &amp; Wrist</h1><p class="subtitle">Prototype CAD and Hardware Integration Planning</p><p class="hero-intro">Developing a modular gripper with compliant fingers, servo linkages, and a bearing-supported wrist around measured component interfaces.</p><div class="research-meta"><span>Fall 2026 – Present</span><span>Duke University · Manipulation project</span></div><p class="status">In progress · CAD prepared for prototype fit checks</p><div class="hero-actions"><a class="button primary" href="#contributions">My contributions ↓</a><a class="button" href="#interfaces">Design details ↓</a></div></div><figure class="evidence-figure "><a href="/images/projects/gripper-wrist/01_three_finger_prototype.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Open Three-finger gripper and wrist CAD at full resolution"><img src="/images/projects/gripper-wrist/01_three_finger_prototype.png" alt="Three-finger gripper and wrist CAD" width="1500" height="1100" loading="eager" /></a><figcaption><strong>Three-finger gripper and wrist CAD</strong><span>Design-stage assembly with a removable bench pedestal. This rendering does not demonstrate physical performance.</span></figcaption></figure></section><nav class="page-index" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#contributions">My contributions</a><a href="#interfaces">Interfaces</a><a href="#validation">Next fit checks</a></nav>
<section class="section dark" id="contributions"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">01</p><h2>My Contributions</h2></div><p>Turning actual hardware dimensions into constraints for a prototype assembly.</p></div><div class="contribution-list"><div><span>01</span><p>Measured available hardware and identified fit constraints for the servo, wrist axle, bearings, encoder board, and connecting parts.</p></div><div><span>02</span><p>Iterated the prototype CAD around those constraints, including servo-horn attachment, finger supports, pivot spacing, and encoder mounting.</p></div><div><span>03</span><p>Prepared a staged prototyping approach using interface gauges, a single-finger mechanism, and a bench-mounted wrist before full assembly testing.</p></div></div></section>
<section class="section" id="interfaces"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">02</p><h2>Designing the Interfaces</h2></div><p>The assembly combines compliant contact elements with rigid links, supports, and mounting features.</p></div><div class="evidence-grid"><figure class="evidence-figure "><a href="/images/projects/gripper-wrist/03_servo_and_linkage.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Open Servo and finger linkage CAD at full resolution"><img src="/images/projects/gripper-wrist/03_servo_and_linkage.png" alt="Servo and finger linkage CAD" width="1500" height="1100" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption><strong>Servo and finger linkage CAD</strong><span>Stock servo-horn attachment, a connecting link, and a rocker drive the compliant finger. The finger and linkage still require physical motion checks.</span></figcaption></figure><figure class="evidence-figure "><a href="/images/projects/gripper-wrist/04_wrist_and_encoder.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Open Wrist and encoder mounting CAD at full resolution"><img src="/images/projects/gripper-wrist/04_wrist_and_encoder.png" alt="Wrist and encoder mounting CAD" width="1500" height="1100" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption><strong>Wrist and encoder mounting CAD</strong><span>Bearing-supported wrist, encoder carrier, and removable bench pedestal. Board outline measurements inform the carrier; encoder operation is not yet validated.</span></figcaption></figure></div><div class="method-grid evidence-findings"><article><h3>Servo attachment</h3><p>The design retains the supplied horn and provides a slotted crank and match-drill option. Physical alignment and retention are fit-check priorities.</p></article><article><h3>Pivot spacing</h3><p>Separate links, spacers, and support geometry allow the moving joints to be checked before the remaining fingers are assembled.</p></article><article><h3>Wrist mounting</h3><p>The bench pedestal allows wrist assembly work before committing to an arm-tip interface. Shaft and bearing dimensions remain explicit design inputs.</p></article></div></section>
<section class="section" id="validation"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">03</p><h2>Prototype Fit Checks</h2></div><p>Check individual interfaces before evaluating the complete mechanism.</p></div><figure class="evidence-figure "><a href="/images/projects/gripper-wrist/05_updated_parts_and_fit_checks.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Open Interface gauges and prototype mounting parts at full resolution"><img src="/images/projects/gripper-wrist/05_updated_parts_and_fit_checks.png" alt="Interface gauges and prototype mounting parts" width="1500" height="1100" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption><strong>Interface gauges and prototype mounting parts</strong><span>CAD views of optional fit gauges and revised components. Printed fit-test results are not yet reported.</span></figcaption></figure><p class="evidence-summary">The planned sequence starts with bearing/shaft and servo-fit gauges, then one side-finger mechanism, followed by wrist assembly and full-system integration. Motion clearance, encoder alignment, and repeated gripping need physical evaluation.</p><div class="callout"><strong>Design scope</strong><p>The compliant fingers follow a Fin-Ray-style concept. This page documents the gripper and wrist development; the separate <a href="/projects/continuum-manipulator/">Continuum Manipulator lifting-dynamics study</a> has its own objectives. Load capacity, sensing accuracy, and grasp reliability are not yet established.</p></div></section><ProjectNavigation slug="gripper-wrist" /></main><SiteFooter /></body></html>